{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"examples","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Examples"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Want to skip to the implementation? Check out these Octane examples:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../examples/column-ordering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-ordering-setup","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering Setup"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's how you set up your table to use column ordering features. Adding the column ordering feature enables the related APIs."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"column-ordering-octane-guide","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering (Octane) Guide"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"By default, columns are ordered in the order they are defined in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" array. However, you can manually specify the column order using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state. Other features like column pinning and grouping can also affect the column order."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"what-affects-column-order","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What Affects Column Order"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are 3 table features that can reorder columns, which happen in the following order:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./column-pinning","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Pinning"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - If pinning, columns are split into start, center (unpinned), and end pinned columns."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Manual "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - A manually specified column order is applied."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./grouping","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grouping"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - If grouping is enabled, a grouping state is active, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tableOptions.groupedColumnMode"},{"type":"text","value":" is set to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'reorder' | 'remove'"},{"type":"text","value":", then the grouped columns are reordered to the start of the column flow."}]}]}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state will only affect unpinned columns if used in conjunction with column pinning."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-order-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Order State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you don't provide a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state, TanStack Table will just use the order of the columns in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columns"},{"type":"text","value":" array. However, you can provide an array of string column ids to the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state to specify the order of the columns."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"default-column-order","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default Column Order"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If all you need to do is specify the initial column order, you can just specify the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state in the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" table option."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  initialState: {\n    columnOrder: ['columnId1', 'columnId2', 'columnId3'],\n  },\n  //...\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Note","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you are using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" table option to also specify the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" will have no effect. Only specify particular states in either "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":", not both."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"managing-column-order-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Managing Column Order State"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you need to dynamically change the column order, or set the column order after the table has been initialized, you can manage the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state just like any other table state."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In v9, the recommended way to own a state slice is with an external atom passed to the table's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"atoms"},{"type":"text","value":" option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can read or write the column order without re-rendering the component that owns the table."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/octane-store'\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\nimport type { ColumnOrderState } from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst columnOrderAtom = useCreateAtom<ColumnOrderState>([\n  'columnId1',\n  'columnId2',\n  'columnId3',\n])\n\nconst columnOrder = useSelector(columnOrderAtom) // subscribe wherever it is needed\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  atoms: {\n    columnOrder: columnOrderAtom,\n  },\n  //...\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Alternatively, the v8-style "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state.columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" plus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onColumnOrderChange"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./table-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table State Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for a deeper comparison."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst [columnOrder, setColumnOrder] = useState<ColumnOrderState>([\n  'columnId1',\n  'columnId2',\n  'columnId3',\n])\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    columnOrder,\n    //...\n  },\n  onColumnOrderChange: setColumnOrder,\n  //...\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"reordering-columns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reordering Columns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the table has UI that allows the user to reorder columns, hook the drop event of your drag-and-drop solution up to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":". With native browser drag events, track which column is being dragged and splice it into place when it is dropped on a target column:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useState } from 'octane/hooks'\n\nconst [draggedColumnId, setDraggedColumnId] = useState<string | null>(null)\n\n// reorder columns after drag & drop\nconst handleDrop = (targetColumnId: string) => {\n  if (!draggedColumnId || draggedColumnId === targetColumnId) return\n  table.setColumnOrder((prevColumnOrder) => {\n    const newColumnOrder = [...prevColumnOrder]\n    const [movedColumnId] = newColumnOrder.splice(\n      newColumnOrder.indexOf(draggedColumnId),\n      1,\n    )\n    newColumnOrder.splice(\n      newColumnOrder.indexOf(targetColumnId),\n      0,\n      movedColumnId,\n    )\n    return newColumnOrder\n  })\n  setDraggedColumnId(null)\n}\n\n// wire up onDragStart={() => setDraggedColumnId(header.column.id)},\n// onDragOver={(e) => e.preventDefault()}, and\n// onDrop={() => handleDrop(header.column.id)} on your <th> elements","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you use a drag-and-drop library instead, call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" from its drop callback in the same way (most libraries provide an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"arrayMove"},{"type":"text","value":"-style utility for the splice logic)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" works the same whether the table manages the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" state internally, you control it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"state"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onColumnOrderChange"},{"type":"text","value":", or you own it with an external atom."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"column-ordering-apis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column Ordering APIs"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" to update the column order state directly. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.resetColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" to reset the order to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState.columnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":", or pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" to clear the order state."}]},{"type":"code","value":"table.setColumnOrder(['lastName', 'firstName', 'age'])\ntable.resetColumnOrder()\ntable.resetColumnOrder(true)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Columns expose helpers for reading their current position after column pinning, manual ordering, and grouping have been applied."}]},{"type":"code","value":"column.getIndex()\ncolumn.getIndex('start')\ncolumn.getIndex('center')\ncolumn.getIndex('end')\n\ncolumn.getIsFirstColumn()\ncolumn.getIsLastColumn()","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"These helpers are useful for styling column boundaries or building drag-and-drop targets that need to know the current rendered order."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":4,"id":"drag-and-drop-column-reordering-suggestions-octane","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Drag and Drop Column Reordering Suggestions (Octane)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Table is not opinionated about which drag-and-drop solution you use. There is no official Octane DnD example yet, but here are a few suggestions:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"loose":true,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Native browser drag events ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDragStart"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDragOver"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onDrop"},{"type":"text","value":") with a little of your own state are often the simplest fit for Octane. This approach has zero dependencies and works directly with Octane's event handling, but you will need to do extra work for proper touch support on mobile. "},{"type":"link","href":"https://www.material-react-table.com/docs/examples/column-ordering","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Material React Table"}]},{"type":"text","value":" implements TanStack Table column ordering this way with no DnD dependencies, and its splice logic translates directly to Octane."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/about","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pragmatic drag and drop"}]},{"type":"text","value":" has a framework-agnostic core that attaches to plain DOM elements, so it works in Octane apps without any compatibility layer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The official React "},{"type":"link","href":"https://tanstack.com/table/latest/docs/framework/react/examples/column-dnd","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Column DnD example"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is a useful reference for the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"arrayMove"},{"type":"text","value":" + "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"table.setColumnOrder"},{"type":"text","value":" pattern, but its React-specific DnD dependency is not an Octane recommendation and the example is not ported."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you evaluate another DnD library, verify that it supports framework-agnostic DOM integration, touch and keyboard input, and semantic "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"<table>"},{"type":"text","value":" markup before committing to it."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"examples","text":"Examples","level":2},{"id":"column-ordering-setup","text":"Column Ordering Setup","level":3},{"id":"column-ordering-octane-guide","text":"Column Ordering (Octane) Guide","level":2},{"id":"what-affects-column-order","text":"What Affects Column Order","level":3},{"id":"column-order-state","text":"Column Order State","level":3},{"id":"default-column-order","text":"Default Column Order","level":4},{"id":"managing-column-order-state","text":"Managing Column Order State","level":4},{"id":"reordering-columns","text":"Reordering Columns","level":3},{"id":"column-ordering-apis","text":"Column Ordering APIs","level":3},{"id":"drag-and-drop-column-reordering-suggestions-octane","text":"Drag and Drop Column Reordering Suggestions (Octane)","level":4}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Octane examples:\n\n- [Column Ordering](../examples/column-ordering)\n\n### Column Ordering Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use column ordering features. Adding the column ordering feature enables the related APIs.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n## Column Ordering (Octane) Guide\n\nBy default, columns are ordered in the order they are defined in the `columns` array. However, you can manually specify the column order using the `columnOrder` state. Other features like column pinning and grouping can also affect the column order.\n\n### What Affects Column Order\n\nThere are 3 table features that can reorder columns, which happen in the following order:\n\n1. [Column Pinning](./column-pinning) - If pinning, columns are split into start, center (unpinned), and end pinned columns.\n2. Manual **Column Ordering** - A manually specified column order is applied.\n3. [Grouping](./grouping) - If grouping is enabled, a grouping state is active, and `tableOptions.groupedColumnMode` is set to `'reorder' | 'remove'`, then the grouped columns are reordered to the start of the column flow.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `columnOrder` state will only affect unpinned columns if used in conjunction with column pinning.\n\n### Column Order State\n\nIf you don't provide a `columnOrder` state, TanStack Table will just use the order of the columns in the `columns` array. However, you can provide an array of string column ids to the `columnOrder` state to specify the order of the columns.\n\n#### Default Column Order\n\nIf all you need to do is specify the initial column order, you can just specify the `columnOrder` state in the `initialState` table option.\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  initialState: {\n    columnOrder: ['columnId1', 'columnId2', 'columnId3'],\n  },\n  //...\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> If you are using the `state` table option to also specify the `columnOrder` state, the `initialState` will have no effect. Only specify particular states in either `initialState` or `state`, not both.\n\n#### Managing Column Order State\n\nIf you need to dynamically change the column order, or set the column order after the table has been initialized, you can manage the `columnOrder` state just like any other table state.\n\nIn v9, the recommended way to own a state slice is with an external atom passed to the table's `atoms` option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can read or write the column order without re-rendering the component that owns the table.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/octane-store'\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\nimport type { ColumnOrderState } from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst columnOrderAtom = useCreateAtom<ColumnOrderState>([\n  'columnId1',\n  'columnId2',\n  'columnId3',\n])\n\nconst columnOrder = useSelector(columnOrderAtom) // subscribe wherever it is needed\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  atoms: {\n    columnOrder: columnOrderAtom,\n  },\n  //...\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.columnOrder` plus `onColumnOrderChange` pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst [columnOrder, setColumnOrder] = useState<ColumnOrderState>([\n  'columnId1',\n  'columnId2',\n  'columnId3',\n])\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    columnOrder,\n    //...\n  },\n  onColumnOrderChange: setColumnOrder,\n  //...\n})\n```\n\n### Reordering Columns\n\nIf the table has UI that allows the user to reorder columns, hook the drop event of your drag-and-drop solution up to `table.setColumnOrder`. With native browser drag events, track which column is being dragged and splice it into place when it is dropped on a target column:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useState } from 'octane/hooks'\n\nconst [draggedColumnId, setDraggedColumnId] = useState<string | null>(null)\n\n// reorder columns after drag & drop\nconst handleDrop = (targetColumnId: string) => {\n  if (!draggedColumnId || draggedColumnId === targetColumnId) return\n  table.setColumnOrder((prevColumnOrder) => {\n    const newColumnOrder = [...prevColumnOrder]\n    const [movedColumnId] = newColumnOrder.splice(\n      newColumnOrder.indexOf(draggedColumnId),\n      1,\n    )\n    newColumnOrder.splice(\n      newColumnOrder.indexOf(targetColumnId),\n      0,\n      movedColumnId,\n    )\n    return newColumnOrder\n  })\n  setDraggedColumnId(null)\n}\n\n// wire up onDragStart={() => setDraggedColumnId(header.column.id)},\n// onDragOver={(e) => e.preventDefault()}, and\n// onDrop={() => handleDrop(header.column.id)} on your <th> elements\n```\n\nIf you use a drag-and-drop library instead, call `table.setColumnOrder` from its drop callback in the same way (most libraries provide an `arrayMove`-style utility for the splice logic).\n\n`table.setColumnOrder` works the same whether the table manages the `columnOrder` state internally, you control it with `state` + `onColumnOrderChange`, or you own it with an external atom.\n\n### Column Ordering APIs\n\nUse `table.setColumnOrder` to update the column order state directly. Use `table.resetColumnOrder` to reset the order to `initialState.columnOrder`, or pass `true` to clear the order state.\n\n```tsx\ntable.setColumnOrder(['lastName', 'firstName', 'age'])\ntable.resetColumnOrder()\ntable.resetColumnOrder(true)\n```\n\nColumns expose helpers for reading their current position after column pinning, manual ordering, and grouping have been applied.\n\n```tsx\ncolumn.getIndex()\ncolumn.getIndex('start')\ncolumn.getIndex('center')\ncolumn.getIndex('end')\n\ncolumn.getIsFirstColumn()\ncolumn.getIsLastColumn()\n```\n\nThese helpers are useful for styling column boundaries or building drag-and-drop targets that need to know the current rendered order.\n\n#### Drag and Drop Column Reordering Suggestions (Octane)\n\nTanStack Table is not opinionated about which drag-and-drop solution you use. There is no official Octane DnD example yet, but here are a few suggestions:\n\n1. Native browser drag events (`onDragStart`, `onDragOver`, `onDrop`) with a little of your own state are often the simplest fit for Octane. This approach has zero dependencies and works directly with Octane's event handling, but you will need to do extra work for proper touch support on mobile. [Material React Table](https://www.material-react-table.com/docs/examples/column-ordering) implements TanStack Table column ordering this way with no DnD dependencies, and its splice logic translates directly to Octane.\n\n2. [Pragmatic drag and drop](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/about) has a framework-agnostic core that attaches to plain DOM elements, so it works in Octane apps without any compatibility layer.\n\n3. The official React [Column DnD example](https://tanstack.com/table/latest/docs/framework/react/examples/column-dnd) is a useful reference for the `arrayMove` + `table.setColumnOrder` pattern, but its React-specific DnD dependency is not an Octane recommendation and the example is not ported.\n\n4. If you evaluate another DnD library, verify that it supports framework-agnostic DOM integration, touch and keyboard input, and semantic `<table>` markup before committing to it.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Column Ordering (Octane) Guide\n---\n\n## Examples\n\nWant to skip to the implementation? Check out these Octane examples:\n\n- [Column Ordering](../examples/column-ordering)\n\n### Column Ordering Setup\n\nHere's how you set up your table to use column ordering features. Adding the column ordering feature enables the related APIs.\n\n```tsx\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  columns,\n  data,\n})\n```\n\n## Column Ordering (Octane) Guide\n\nBy default, columns are ordered in the order they are defined in the `columns` array. However, you can manually specify the column order using the `columnOrder` state. Other features like column pinning and grouping can also affect the column order.\n\n### What Affects Column Order\n\nThere are 3 table features that can reorder columns, which happen in the following order:\n\n1. [Column Pinning](./column-pinning) - If pinning, columns are split into start, center (unpinned), and end pinned columns.\n2. Manual **Column Ordering** - A manually specified column order is applied.\n3. [Grouping](./grouping) - If grouping is enabled, a grouping state is active, and `tableOptions.groupedColumnMode` is set to `'reorder' | 'remove'`, then the grouped columns are reordered to the start of the column flow.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> `columnOrder` state will only affect unpinned columns if used in conjunction with column pinning.\n\n### Column Order State\n\nIf you don't provide a `columnOrder` state, TanStack Table will just use the order of the columns in the `columns` array. However, you can provide an array of string column ids to the `columnOrder` state to specify the order of the columns.\n\n#### Default Column Order\n\nIf all you need to do is specify the initial column order, you can just specify the `columnOrder` state in the `initialState` table option.\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  initialState: {\n    columnOrder: ['columnId1', 'columnId2', 'columnId3'],\n  },\n  //...\n})\n```\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> If you are using the `state` table option to also specify the `columnOrder` state, the `initialState` will have no effect. Only specify particular states in either `initialState` or `state`, not both.\n\n#### Managing Column Order State\n\nIf you need to dynamically change the column order, or set the column order after the table has been initialized, you can manage the `columnOrder` state just like any other table state.\n\nIn v9, the recommended way to own a state slice is with an external atom passed to the table's `atoms` option. External atoms give you fine-grained subscriptions anywhere in your app, and other code can read or write the column order without re-rendering the component that owns the table.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useCreateAtom, useSelector } from '@tanstack/octane-store'\nimport {\n  useTable,\n  tableFeatures,\n  columnOrderingFeature,\n} from '@tanstack/octane-table'\nimport type { ColumnOrderState } from '@tanstack/octane-table'\n\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst columnOrderAtom = useCreateAtom<ColumnOrderState>([\n  'columnId1',\n  'columnId2',\n  'columnId3',\n])\n\nconst columnOrder = useSelector(columnOrderAtom) // subscribe wherever it is needed\n\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  atoms: {\n    columnOrder: columnOrderAtom,\n  },\n  //...\n})\n```\n\nAlternatively, the v8-style `state.columnOrder` plus `onColumnOrderChange` pattern is still supported. It can be convenient for simple integrations or when migrating v8 code, but it is less fine-grained than external atoms. See the [Table State Guide](./table-state) for a deeper comparison.\n\n```tsx\nconst features = tableFeatures({ columnOrderingFeature })\n\nconst [columnOrder, setColumnOrder] = useState<ColumnOrderState>([\n  'columnId1',\n  'columnId2',\n  'columnId3',\n])\n//...\nconst table = useTable({\n  features,\n  //...\n  state: {\n    columnOrder,\n    //...\n  },\n  onColumnOrderChange: setColumnOrder,\n  //...\n})\n```\n\n### Reordering Columns\n\nIf the table has UI that allows the user to reorder columns, hook the drop event of your drag-and-drop solution up to `table.setColumnOrder`. With native browser drag events, track which column is being dragged and splice it into place when it is dropped on a target column:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useState } from 'octane/hooks'\n\nconst [draggedColumnId, setDraggedColumnId] = useState<string | null>(null)\n\n// reorder columns after drag & drop\nconst handleDrop = (targetColumnId: string) => {\n  if (!draggedColumnId || draggedColumnId === targetColumnId) return\n  table.setColumnOrder((prevColumnOrder) => {\n    const newColumnOrder = [...prevColumnOrder]\n    const [movedColumnId] = newColumnOrder.splice(\n      newColumnOrder.indexOf(draggedColumnId),\n      1,\n    )\n    newColumnOrder.splice(\n      newColumnOrder.indexOf(targetColumnId),\n      0,\n      movedColumnId,\n    )\n    return newColumnOrder\n  })\n  setDraggedColumnId(null)\n}\n\n// wire up onDragStart={() => setDraggedColumnId(header.column.id)},\n// onDragOver={(e) => e.preventDefault()}, and\n// onDrop={() => handleDrop(header.column.id)} on your <th> elements\n```\n\nIf you use a drag-and-drop library instead, call `table.setColumnOrder` from its drop callback in the same way (most libraries provide an `arrayMove`-style utility for the splice logic).\n\n`table.setColumnOrder` works the same whether the table manages the `columnOrder` state internally, you control it with `state` + `onColumnOrderChange`, or you own it with an external atom.\n\n### Column Ordering APIs\n\nUse `table.setColumnOrder` to update the column order state directly. Use `table.resetColumnOrder` to reset the order to `initialState.columnOrder`, or pass `true` to clear the order state.\n\n```tsx\ntable.setColumnOrder(['lastName', 'firstName', 'age'])\ntable.resetColumnOrder()\ntable.resetColumnOrder(true)\n```\n\nColumns expose helpers for reading their current position after column pinning, manual ordering, and grouping have been applied.\n\n```tsx\ncolumn.getIndex()\ncolumn.getIndex('start')\ncolumn.getIndex('center')\ncolumn.getIndex('end')\n\ncolumn.getIsFirstColumn()\ncolumn.getIsLastColumn()\n```\n\nThese helpers are useful for styling column boundaries or building drag-and-drop targets that need to know the current rendered order.\n\n#### Drag and Drop Column Reordering Suggestions (Octane)\n\nTanStack Table is not opinionated about which drag-and-drop solution you use. There is no official Octane DnD example yet, but here are a few suggestions:\n\n1. Native browser drag events (`onDragStart`, `onDragOver`, `onDrop`) with a little of your own state are often the simplest fit for Octane. This approach has zero dependencies and works directly with Octane's event handling, but you will need to do extra work for proper touch support on mobile. [Material React Table](https://www.material-react-table.com/docs/examples/column-ordering) implements TanStack Table column ordering this way with no DnD dependencies, and its splice logic translates directly to Octane.\n\n2. [Pragmatic drag and drop](https://atlassian.design/components/pragmatic-drag-and-drop/about) has a framework-agnostic core that attaches to plain DOM elements, so it works in Octane apps without any compatibility layer.\n\n3. The official React [Column DnD example](https://tanstack.com/table/latest/docs/framework/react/examples/column-dnd) is a useful reference for the `arrayMove` + `table.setColumnOrder` pattern, but its React-specific DnD dependency is not an Octane recommendation and the example is not ported.\n\n4. If you evaluate another DnD library, verify that it supports framework-agnostic DOM integration, touch and keyboard input, and semantic `<table>` markup before committing to it.\n"}
